Translating your brand into a comprehensible, approachable, engaging, and generally likable asset is one of the most difficult things to do, if you're lacking creative strategy and vision. However, it's one of the most pivotal moves when it comes to unlock real wins. Once you know your persona, your product, and your brand, you can begin crafting ads that actually mean something to each point of that traingle, and ultimately, begin shaping brand-native creative formats that can become 1) recognizable 2) extremely valuable at scale. 

When evaluating multiple industries, it's rare to find a brand that has one true "brand format"
Repeating a creative template is not the same as develping a brand format. This also isn't to say that brand formats must be your ride or die, but they're unique hard-to-copy creative styles that can make you more recognizable to viewers. 

There are levels to this, and while I'm actively uncovering the depths, there are a few that have surfaced during my research rabbit holes: 
1. Distinct brand format: this summarizes brand creatives that represent repeatable winning structures that are tailored to their product and brand experience. 
2. Brand character: This reflects casting choices that are consistent across brand assets. 
3. Brand tone/voice: This reflects the general vibe or affect of your ad, particularly when it is consistent across all creatives produced. Most brands rely on a consistent voice, and don't climb further up this ladder. 

What can a distinct brand format lead to? 
1. A content or creative series
2. A unique lens from which you pitch your product

If you have an extremely unique hook that has worked well across channels, can you test that hook with other people and drive the same impact? If you've seen a concept scale, can you transform the featured person into a "character" and test different products/angles with the same person/pov, or even expand the concept to another persona/expert/ambassador. 

How to begin to identify the patterns that unlock unique brand formats:
1. Consider the script you keep recasting
2. Outline a repeatable role, rather than a single representative
